From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fingerprint Lab <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 01:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] goodix550c: stop OpenSSL installing an atexit handler

Installed for the host fprintd, the daemon dumped core on shutdown after any
session that had opened the sensor:

    #4  OPENSSL_cleanup      (libcrypto.so.3)
    #5  __run_exit_handlers  (libc.so.6)
    #6  __GI_exit            (libc.so.6)

with three GLib worker threads still live in the same dump. This driver is the
only thing in fprintd that touches OpenSSL, and SSL_CTX_new initializes it
implicitly, which registers OPENSSL_cleanup with atexit(). The handler then
runs during the host's exit, tearing OpenSSL state down underneath threads that
have not stopped yet.

The crash lands after the action completes, so authentication itself was never
affected and D-Bus activation restarted the daemon on the next request. It
still filed a crash report each time and left systemd recording the unit as
failed, which is not something an authentication daemon should be doing.

A driver loaded into somebody else's process should not install a process-wide
exit handler at all. Initialize OpenSSL explicitly with OPENSSL_INIT_NO_ATEXIT
before the first implicit initialization can happen. Nothing else changes: the
flag only declines the handler, and the session teardown this driver already
does at device close is unaffected.

---

--- a/drivers/goodix53x5/goodix53x5-tls.c
+++ b/drivers/goodix53x5/goodix53x5-tls.c
@@ -42,9 +42,36 @@
 /* ex_data index used to retrieve the GoodixTls* inside the PSK callback. */
 static int goodix_tls_ex_index = -1;
 
+/*
+ * OpenSSL registers OPENSSL_cleanup with atexit() the first time anything
+ * initializes it implicitly, and that handler then runs from the host
+ * process's exit path, long after this driver is done with it. fprintd still
+ * has live GLib worker threads at that point, and tearing OpenSSL down under
+ * them segfaults the daemon inside OPENSSL_cleanup on every shutdown that
+ * opened the sensor.
+ *
+ * A driver loaded into somebody else's process has no business installing a
+ * process-wide exit handler, so initialize explicitly and decline it. This has
+ * to happen before any other OpenSSL call, because implicit initialization
+ * runs once and the flags of whoever arrives first are the ones that stick.
+ */
+static void
+goodix_tls_ensure_openssl (void)
+{
+  static gboolean openssl_initialized = FALSE;
+
+  if (openssl_initialized)
+    return;
+
+  openssl_initialized = TRUE;
+  OPENSSL_init_ssl (OPENSSL_INIT_NO_ATEXIT, NULL);
+}
+
 static void
 goodix_tls_ensure_ex_index (void)
 {
+  goodix_tls_ensure_openssl ();
+
   if (goodix_tls_ex_index == -1)
     goodix_tls_ex_index =
       SSL_get_ex_new_index (0, (void *) "GoodixTls", NULL, NULL, NULL);

